extends _blog.pug

block append subtitle
  | Progress report #31

block content
  h2 Progress report #31
  +news_header('27 July 2026', 'report_30', '')

  h3 Shipping to the United States of America again

  p In the morning of Friday, Aug 22, 2025 we were fulfilling orders to the U.S., as usual, when we were greeted by a generic error message upon using the postal service. We first assumed error on our side, attempting to enter the information again. Then we assumed that there was a problem with the Posti back-end, so we tried again later.
  p During a discussion with friend after lunch we came to discuss the Trump administration's decision of July 30, 2025, when the U.S. announced the end of the de-minimis exception, exempting orders of less then USD 800,- from tariffs and import taxes.
  p As it turned out, this change would effect on August 29, 2025, and one week prior, all postal services would stop accepting shipments to the U.S. We contacted other logistics companies, such as UPS and DHL, but to no avail.
  p Hoping that this would last only a few days, or perhaps weeks, we contacted those U.S. customers, explaining the situation and offering a refund.
  p Weeks turned into months, and in December the Spanish postal service announces that it would begin shipping parcels to the U.S. again. When Marcos went home for the holidays, he took one package with him and was able to successfully ship it to the US.
  p While the additional 15% tariffs (plus a service fee) represented a significant cost increase, it was also necessary to provide the customers social security number (SSN). The result of this experiment was that we would be able to serve our U.S. customers again, we decided that it was unreasonable to ask them for their SSN.

  p In June 2026 we signed an agreement with Deutsche Post / DHL, which would enable us to ship to the U.S. again.
  p The cost of shipping with DHL is unfortunately significantly higher than with the normal postal service.
  p Additionally, in order to adapt to the changing U.S. tariff situation, the service requires so-called "Delivered At Place" (DAP), requiring the customer to pay U.S. tariffs and service fees, before final delivery.
  p Last, but not least, the U.S. Customs and Border Patrol (CBP) requires DHL to collect the recipients Employer Identification Number (EIN). However, based on our experience so far, this appears to be optional.

  p So far we have been able to successfully ship about a dozen orders to our U.S. customers. Based on their experience, the additional cost for DAP seem to be in the range of 29,- to 32,- USD.

  hr

  p Thanks for the support! 🤍 <br>- MM (Marcos and Michael)